<section class="section" id="conclusions">
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="section-number">6</span>Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Key Findings</h3>
    <ol style="margin-left: 20px;">
      <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>High structural stability with a single pivot point.</strong> The BIEK CS exam uses a fixed framework of 15 MCQs and 3 Long questions. Only the Short question count has changed — from 9 (2015–16) to 18 (2017–19) to 15 (2021–25). Once established, each format is maintained for multiple consecutive years.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>The 2017 expansion was the most significant structural change.</strong> A 33.3% increase in total questions, entirely from doubling the Short section. This was the largest single-year change in the dataset and reshaped the exam's proportional balance away from MCQ dominance.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>Post-pandemic normalization to a balanced format.</strong> Since 2021, MCQs and Short questions have equal weight (45.5% each), suggesting the exam has found a stable equilibrium.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>Topic consistency is high but evolution is underway.</strong> Core topics (operators, functions, I/O, MS-Access) appear every single year. However, 2024–2025 papers show new topics — normalization, ER diagrams, ACID properties, structures/unions, pointers — signaling a curriculum broadening.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>C programming dominates at ~2:1 over database content.</strong> This ratio is consistent across all years, providing a clear signal for preparation time allocation.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
    <div class="warning-box">
      <ul style="margin-left: 18px;">
        <li><strong>Missing 2020 data:</strong> No examination questions exist for 2020, most likely due to the COVID-19 pandemic. This creates a gap in trend analysis between the pre- and post-pandemic eras.</li>
        <li><strong>No annual vs. supplementary distinction:</strong> The dataset does not separate annual and supplementary examination papers. Some years may include questions from both sessions, potentially inflating counts for those years.</li>
        <li><strong>Community-compiled data:</strong> Questions were manually transcribed from scanned papers. Minor transcription errors may exist, though the overall quality appears high.</li>
        <li><strong>Topic analysis is keyword-based:</strong> The recurring topics analysis uses pattern matching on question text. A question may be classified under multiple topics, and some nuanced topics may be under-represented if they lack distinctive keywords.</li>
        <li><strong>Small dataset:</strong> With 327 rows across 10 years, fine-grained statistical analysis (e.g., per-topic per-year trends) has limited statistical power. Broad patterns are reliable; year-specific anomalies should be interpreted cautiously.</li>
        <li><strong>Theory only:</strong> The dataset covers Paper-II (theory) only. Practical examination patterns are not represented.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Answering the Central Question</h3>
    <div class="insight">
      <strong>Has the BIEK Computer Science exam structure remained stable, or have meaningful shifts occurred?</strong><br><br>
      The exam is <em>fundamentally stable</em> — the MCQ and Long question counts have never changed, core topics recur every year, and the C-vs-Database balance has been maintained. However, <strong>two meaningful shifts</strong> have occurred: (1) the 2017 Short question expansion reshaped the exam balance away from MCQ dominance, and (2) post-2022 papers show early signs of curriculum modernization through the introduction of advanced database and C programming topics. Educators should respond to the second shift proactively by ensuring coverage of normalization, ER modeling, and advanced C constructs.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

